---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/privy-vs-postscript';
const title = 'Privy vs Postscript for Shopify: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = "Compare Privy and Postscript for Shopify signup ownership, SMS operations, channel coordination and the cost of a combined stack.";

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does Postscript require Privy for subscriber capture?",
    "answer": "No. Postscript documents its own popup and checkout collection tools. Retain a separate capture app only for a demonstrated requirement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Privy handle more than popups?",
    "answer": "Its Shopify listing advertises email automation and SMS as well as popups. Compare the current offering with the actual channel requirements."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should overlapping messages be controlled?",
    "answer": "Assign owners and write purchase exclusions, opt-out handling and channel contact rules. Verify the intended behavior across the apps rather than assuming automatic coordination."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">Privy vs Postscript</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Privy vs Postscript for Shopify</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">Choose the roles before choosing the apps. Privy advertises email, SMS and popups; Postscript provides SMS marketing and its own subscriber collection tools. A separate capture app is therefore a design choice, not a prerequisite for using Postscript. The comparison should establish who owns signup, email, text and the handoffs between them.</p>
      </header>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <h2>Correct the capture assumption</h2>
        <p>Postscript documents website popups, checkout collection and other opt-in surfaces. Its developer documentation also describes popup collection through its SDK. Do not purchase Privy solely because someone says Postscript cannot collect subscribers. Privy may still be useful if its combined capture and email workflow fits the store, or if an existing form performs a job the team wants to retain. Demonstrate that job with the required fields and design rather than relying on a categorical winner.</p>
        <h2>Draw two viable stack designs</h2>
        <p>One candidate is Privy for capture and email with its SMS offering evaluated against the same text requirements. Another is Postscript for SMS and its collection tools, with an explicitly chosen email sender. A third-party capture layer can be retained where it provides a necessary experience, but each extra handoff adds configuration and monitoring. List the apps that remain in each design, the automations each sends and the person responsible for the full subscriber path. Do not assume either design is cheaper or operationally simpler until the paths are demonstrated.</p>
        <h2>Proposed limited-release signup exercise</h2>
        <p>Consider a hypothetical apparel release where visitors can request email news, SMS release alerts, or both. This is an unrun exercise. Draw separate permission paths for each choice. Use controlled profiles to inspect the stored signup source and status in the intended sender. Postscript documents subscriber-source tracking; use that information to distinguish popup and checkout entries. Confirm what happens when a known email subscriber adds SMS later. Collecting a phone number must not be treated as permission to send every channel or every message type.</p>
        <h2>Coordinate launch and cart messages</h2>
        <p>Write a release sequence with the email announcement, SMS alert and cart follow-up on one timeline. Determine which message is withheld after purchase and which operator can pause the release. Then propose a controlled test of purchase before the scheduled reminder, an opt-out before the next send and a customer reply about sizing. Postscript advertises reply forwarding and help-desk connections; demonstrate the chosen destination and staff ownership. A message arriving in an unattended inbox is an operational gap even if the integration technically connects.</p>
        <h2>Evaluate permission controls in context</h2>
        <p>Review the actual collection copy, destination markets, opt-out path and account settings with the people responsible for the program. Postscript documents onsite confirmation behavior, but product tooling does not establish that a particular store has met every obligation. Do not claim either platform guarantees compliance or that one fixed delay between email and SMS is universally safe. Choose contact rules for the intended customer experience, then inspect how those rules are implemented across apps.</p>
        <h2>Model SMS as an operating cost</h2>
        <p>Use the same planned recipient countries, message content, volume and cadence in both quotes. Record plan fees, usage charges, carrier-related charges where applicable, support scope and additional email costs. Ask how media, message segmentation and failed sends affect billing. Include staff time for creative approval and replies. Attributed SMS revenue does not by itself prove incremental profit; overlapping email and text may both claim the same purchase. A proposed holdout requires a suitable design and enough observations, not an invented revenue-share threshold.</p>
        <h2>Choose a role configuration that can be maintained</h2>
        <p>Prefer the design that covers required channels with a clear owner for each signup and send path. Keep Privy if its combined workflow addresses the store requirements; consider Postscript when its demonstrated SMS collection and operation fit those requirements. Keep both only when the retained benefit exceeds the added handoff work. Before cutover, save form settings, identify the previous automation owner and write how to restore it without sending a second welcome. No monthly revenue figure determines when a dedicated SMS tool becomes necessary.</p>
        <h2>Primary sources and evaluation limits</h2>
        <p>Reviewed 25 November 2026. These sources establish advertised scope, not measured results or account-specific behavior. Product exercises above are proposed and unrun; plan availability, quotes and store configuration require confirmation.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://apps.shopify.com/privy">Privy Shopify listing</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/features">Postscript collection and reply tools</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://developers.postscript.io/docs/about-our-sdk">Postscript SDK</a></li>
          <li><a class="link" href="https://help.postscript.io/en/articles/13563865-understanding-subscriber-opt-in-sources">Postscript signup sources</a></li>
        </ul>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-postscript">Klaviyo vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-postscript">Omnisend vs Postscript</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/fashion">Email apps for fashion stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Privy vs Postscript FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
